<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Kiev</h1><p class="st-meta">1132 CE – 1471 CE (approx.) · capital Kiev</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The principality of the old capital of Rus', a prize fought over by rival princes, which later became a Lithuanian vassal state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-i-of-kiev">Mstislav the Great</a> died in 1132, the unity of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> broke down. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> itself and its surrounding lands became one principality among many, though still senior in prestige and the seat of the metropolitan. Princes of the Monomakh and Olgovichi lines competed for its throne.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> changed hands dozens of times in the 12th century. The armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrei-bogolyubsky">Andrei Bogolyubsky</a> sacked it in 1169, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-rostislavich">Rurik Rostislavich</a> and his Cuman allies sacked it again in 1203. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> destroyed the city in 1240, and for a century it was ruled by minor princes and Mongol officials.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1362 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and gave it to his son Volodymyr. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olelkovich">Olelkovich</a> princes it was a Lithuanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state with an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> court. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semen-olelkovich">Semen Olelkovich</a> died in 1470, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV</a> abolished the principality in 1471 and made it a voivodeship.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-kiev.jpg" alt="Principality of Kiev" width="320" height="347"><figcaption>Image: Hellerick · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rus_de_Kiev_en_1237.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1132 CE – 1471 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kiev</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Iziaslav Mstislavich, Volodymyr Olgerdovich, Semen Olelkovich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Kyiv, Kiev Principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1302&amp;focus=principality-of-kiev" aria-label="Principality of Kiev on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5377 425 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Kiev lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5697 681L5697 681M5697 681L7559 780M5697 681L6352 637M5697 681L5556 562"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5697 681h0M7559 780h0M6352 637h0M5556 562h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5697 681h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5697 681h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1302&amp;focus=principality-of-kiev">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
